{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:auth.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the declared failure code `auth` \u2014 one of the four the artifact declares as distinct values for distinct causes, and the vector pins which one reached the verdict. The refusal carries **no condition**: the artifact obliges the peer to close and names an authentication-failure close-code that no value of the generated connection-error choice carries, so there is no condition to report and none is invented. What carries the information is the reason class, which is the code's own declared name read from the `sasl-code` choice \u2014 the class vocabulary grew for this layer by exactly those values and nothing else. The step is refused rather than admitted, because the outcome that reports a failure is the outcome that ends the dialogue.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001002010000005344c003015001", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"reason": "auth", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-auth"}
