{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:sys-perm.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the declared failure code `sys-perm` \u2014 one of the four the artifact declares as distinct values for distinct causes, and the vector pins which one reached the verdict. The refusal carries **no condition**: the artifact obliges the peer to close and names an authentication-failure close-code that no value of the generated connection-error choice carries, so there is no condition to report and none is invented. What carries the information is the reason class, which is the code's own declared name read from the `sasl-code` choice \u2014 the class vocabulary grew for this layer by exactly those values and nothing else. The step is refused rather than admitted, because the outcome that reports a failure is the outcome that ends the dialogue.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001002010000005344c003015003", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"reason": "sys-perm", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-sys-perm"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:sys-temp.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the declared failure code `sys-temp` \u2014 one of the four the artifact declares as distinct values for distinct causes, and the vector pins which one reached the verdict. The refusal carries **no condition**: the artifact obliges the peer to close and names an authentication-failure close-code that no value of the generated connection-error choice carries, so there is no condition to report and none is invented. What carries the information is the reason class, which is the code's own declared name read from the `sasl-code` choice \u2014 the class vocabulary grew for this layer by exactly those values and nothing else. The step is refused rather than admitted, because the outcome that reports a failure is the outcome that ends the dialogue.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001002010000005344c003015004", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"reason": "sys-temp", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-sys-temp"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-init/field:mechanism.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-init/field:mechanism.2", "amqp-core-types-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:types/section:composite-type-representation.2"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the field a `sasl-init` cannot do without, read on the side where the init is permitted: without the mandatory-field guard this was refused as an *unoffered mechanism* under the wire-level condition \u2014 the peer was told its mechanism was unsupported when it had sent none \u2014 and both artefacts agreed on that wrong answer, so no differential over them could see it.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_RCVD", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000000e02010000005341c00100", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}, "note": "the mechanism field is present and null, which the declared surface's mandatory rule counts as unset"}], "vector": "sasl-init-without-mechanism"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.6", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-init/field:mechanism.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the unoffered-mechanism refusal, extended from the exchange corpus's single-mechanism case to a list: the peer announcing PLAIN and EXTERNAL receives an init naming ANONYMOUS, and the membership rule refuses it. The vector is read on the **server's** side deliberately \u2014 the init arrives in the direction the dialogue permits, so only the membership rule can refuse it; the same frame offered on the client's side would be refused for its direction first, and a vector cannot tell two rules apart when both are able to answer with the same condition. The refusal carries the one condition the artifact uses for a wire-level failure, because the close-code the clause names for this (the authentication-failure code) has no value in the generated choice table.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_RCVD", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002202010000005340c01501c01202a305504c41494ea30845585445524e414c", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001902010000005341c00c01a309414e4f4e594d4f5553", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:connection:framing-error", "reason": "unsupported", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-init-mechanism-not-offered-from-a-list"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.6", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.7", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.6"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "who may announce and who may initiate: the peer that sent the mechanisms is the SASL server, and picture.6 gives the init to its partner. A server that inits is refused in the direction the picture forbids, which is the same rule the exchange corpus's challenge-from-the-client vector pins from the other side.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_RCVD", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001c02010000005340c00f01c00c01a309414e4f4e594d4f5553", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001902010000005341c00c01a309414e4f4e594d4f5553", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "refused"}, "note": "a refused send writes nothing and moves nothing, so the peer is left in the state it was in"}], "vector": "sasl-init-from-the-server"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-types-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:types/section:composite-type-representation.2", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.6"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the `challenge` field is mandatory, and the dialogue's own moment and direction both hold here \u2014 the peer is the client and the server is challenging it \u2014 so nothing but the field's presence decides this step. Before the guard the frame was taken as the challenge it names.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000000e02010000005342c00100", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-challenge-without-challenge"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-types-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:types/section:composite-type-representation.2", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.6"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the same rule in the direction the client sends, where the consequence is that nothing is written: a response with no response data is not a response, and the refusal leaves the peer where it stood so the dialogue can continue with the frame the shared layer actually asks for.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001502010000005342c00801a0056e6f6e6365", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000000e02010000005343c00100", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-response-without-response"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the outcome's `code` is mandatory, and its absence used to be read as a *failure*: the code was unset, the establishment test compared nothing with `ok`, and the peer was sent to END as though the server had reported bad credentials. The frame is not an outcome at all, and the refusal now says so.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000000e02010000005344c00100", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-without-code"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:ok.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:auth.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:sys.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:sys-perm.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:sys-temp.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "`sasl-code` is an enumerated field: the choice declares ok, auth, sys, sys-perm and sys-temp, and 9 is none of them. Read as a failure the frame was accepted and closed the connection, which told the peer its outcome was well formed when its code was not a code the artifact defines.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001002010000005344c003015009", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-undeclared-code"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome/field:additional-data.u1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-code/choice:auth.u1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "'If the authentication is unsuccessful, this field is not set' \u2014 a rule about this frame's own content, so a failure outcome that carries additional-data is refused the way every other field-value violation in this layer is. The vector's sibling `sasl-outcome-additional-data-on-success` is what keeps this from being an exclusion: the field is permitted, on the outcomes the artifact permits it on.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001502010000005344c008025001a003626164", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-additional-data-on-failure"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.6", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.6"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the outcome is the server's to send, like the challenge: the peer that received the mechanisms is the client, and it cannot close a dialogue it does not own.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001002010000005344c003015000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-from-the-client"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.7", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-outcome.1"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the dialogue's order: the outcome closes an exchange the init began, and an outcome received while the peer is still waiting for the init is a frame of the right layer in the wrong moment.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001002010000005344c003015000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-outcome-before-init"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.6", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.7"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the announcement happens once: the mechanisms are the server's offer and the init answers them, so a second announcement is not a wider offer \u2014 the dialogue is past choosing and waiting for one.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002302010000005340c01601c01302a305504c41494ea309414e4f4e594d4f5553", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-mechanisms-twice"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl/type:sasl-mechanisms/field:sasl-server-mechanisms.u1", "amqp-core-types-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:types/section:composite-type-representation.3", "amqp-core-types-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:types/section:composite-type-representation.2"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "'It is invalid for this list to be null or empty' \u2014 the null half, and the same value written the other way. The exchange corpus's vector pins the empty half, where the list is present with nothing in it; this one writes the field's item as an explicit null, which the type system makes the same value as omitting it ('a null value and a zero-length array both describe an absence of a value and MUST be treated as semantically identical', and trailing nulls MAY be omitted). A field defined as both multiple and mandatory MUST contain at least one value, so both spellings are refused \u2014 and both are refused as the *same* thing, which is the part a receiver could get wrong by treating the explicit null as a list of one null.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000000f02010000005340c0020140", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:invalid-field", "reason": "malformed", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-mechanisms-null"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:transport/section:connections.2"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the layer-transition rule at the moment after the init: the AMQP layer does not exist until the outcome establishes it and the protocol headers are exchanged again, so the `open` is refused however well formed it is. The exchange corpus pins the same rule earlier, while the dialogue is waiting for the mechanisms; this is the other end of the dialogue, where the peer knows more and the rule is unchanged.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001802010000005340c00b01c00801a305504c41494e", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000002602010000005341c01902a305504c41494ea00f006175746863696400706173737764", "direction": "send", "expect": {"status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001602000000005310c00901a106636c69656e74", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-amqp-frame-sent-after-init"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:transport/section:connections.2"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the same rule in the receive direction, and the placement is the point: the refusal is a receive in the SASL layer, where no AMQP close can be written because the layer is not established, so the transport is cut \u2014 END, not the AMQP layer's DISCARDING.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001602000000005310c00901a106736572766572", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:END", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-amqp-frame-received-before-mechanisms"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:transport/section:connections.2"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the rule is about the layer, not about one performative: a `close` is as much an AMQP-layer frame as an `open`, and a peer that cannot open cannot close either \u2014 there is no connection to close until the layer is established, and a peer that wants out of the dialogue cuts the transport.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_SENT", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000000e02000000005318c00100", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-close-in-the-sasl-layer"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the other half of the layer rule, in the direction the exchange corpus does not have: the same SASL performative that corpus refuses on the way in is refused on the way out, because a peer that did not negotiate protocol id three has no dialogue to send it into.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515000010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_RCVD", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515000010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "0000001c02010000005340c00f01c00c01a309414e4f4e594d4f5553", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:illegal-state", "reason": "illegalState", "state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "refused"}}], "vector": "sasl-frame-sent-in-the-amqp-layer"}
{"clauses": ["amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#picture.24", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#picture.3", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.4", "amqp-core-security-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:security/section:sasl.1", "amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:transport/section:version-negotiation.1", "amqp-core-transport-v1.0-os.xml#amqp:transport/section:version-negotiation.3"], "kind": "exchange", "note": "the send-side half of the header-mismatch rule. The peer received a SASL header, so the layer of this exchange is chosen \u2014 version negotiation's own rule, that the header exchange is what selects the protocol \u2014 and a peer that answers with an AMQP header instead would land in the AMQP layer while its partner is in the SASL dialogue. Before this guard the send was *admitted*, and every exchange vector reached the header step from START, where either layer is free, so the asymmetry survived a corpus that tested the direction that worked.", "start": "connection:START", "steps": [{"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "receive", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_RCVD", "status": "admitted"}}, {"bytes": "414d515000010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"condition": "amqp:connection:framing-error", "reason": "unsupported", "state": "connection:HDR_RCVD", "status": "refused"}, "note": "a refused send moves nothing, so the peer is still in HDR_RCVD in the SASL layer and can send the header the exchange actually is"}, {"bytes": "414d515003010000", "direction": "send", "expect": {"state": "connection:HDR_EXCH", "status": "admitted"}, "note": "and the dialogue proceeds with the header the moment asks for"}], "vector": "sasl-header-switch-on-send"}
